<h2>Taking photos and video that help</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shoot first, clean second.</strong> Photograph everything before moving it, then again after emergency mitigation.</li>
<li><strong>Use three distances.</strong> A wide shot to show where you are, a mid-range shot of the area, and close-ups of the damage itself.</li>
<li><strong>Add a scale reference.</strong> A coin, tape measure, or ruler beside hail, dents, or cracks makes size obvious.</li>
<li><strong>Circle the whole property.</strong> Capture every side of the house plus fences, sheds, carports, barns, and vehicles. Hail driven by wind often damages one elevation far more than the others.</li>
<li><strong>Narrate a walkthrough.</strong> Say the date, the address, and what you are looking at. Video ties individual photos together.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the originals.</strong> Do not crop or edit the original files; the embedded date and time can matter.</li>
<li><strong>Do not take risks.</strong> Stay off wet or damaged roofs and out of floodwater. Photograph from the ground and let a professional inspect the roof.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Setting up your claim file</h2>
<p>Keep one folder, on paper or in the cloud, that you can reach from your phone. A simple structure:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policy documents:</strong> full policy, declarations page, endorsements, and any separate flood policy.</li>
<li><strong>Claim details:</strong> claim number, date of loss, and the names and contact details of every adjuster.</li>
<li><strong>Photos and video,</strong> sorted by date and area.</li>
<li><strong>Weather records:</strong> reports for your date of loss from the <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> or the <a href="/weather-events/">recent weather events</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Estimates and receipts:</strong> contractor bids, the insurer’s estimate, mitigation invoices, permits, and hotel or meal receipts if you were displaced.</li>
<li><strong>Contents inventory:</strong> damaged belongings or business property with descriptions, ages, and costs where known.</li>
<li><strong>Correspondence:</strong> every letter, email, and text, plus your call log.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Keeping a claim communication log</h2>
<p>Record every call, email, and visit: the date and time, the person’s name and role, what was said, and any next steps or deadlines. After an important phone call, send a short email confirming what was discussed. Texas prompt-payment rules give insurers set timeframes to acknowledge, investigate, and decide claims, and a clear log shows when each request and response really happened. That can be useful if your claim becomes <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed</a>.</p>

